{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's terms as a procedural rule of rational action, not an inductive inference about truth probability. It costs us the ability to claim the preferred theory is 'more likely true,' restricting us to acting on the theory that has best withstood attempts at falsification so far.","counterconditions":["If the agent assumes past survival guarantees future success, the defense collapses into induction.","If the preference is framed as increasing the theory's truth-content probability rather than its status as the best available conjecture."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Distinction between logical justification (probability) and pragmatic rationality (action-guiding rules). The mechanism relies on the 'principle of transference' being rejected: just because we act on X doesn't mean we believe X is probable. We act on it because it is the only non-irrational choice given the absence of refutation.","optional_refs":["09292130faa932cf0804d2bc6f0ea0385aa2f8e73b576128b38110b22e56297b","f3d844769f6a642144498ae246a7113529c8b3452ba77763a83abac7ae2ed13f"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories inevitably smuggles in induction. Any claim that a theory is 'better' or 'preferred' due to past test survival implicitly assumes the uniformity of nature—that the future will resemble the past—which is the very principle Popper sought to eliminate.","counterconditions":["If one can demonstrate a purely deductive link between test survival and future reliability without assuming uniformity.","If the preference is shown to be arbitrary or random, undermining the 'better' qualifier."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Hidden Premise Extraction. The mechanism identifies the tacit assumption required to move from 'Theory T survived tests t1...tn' to 'Prefer T for future action.' This step requires the inductive leap that surviving tests makes a theory a reliable guide for the future.","optional_refs":["aac299819be57013d012f722e49c41f34742bfc3733db55a6e2d2debc6c8a4b5","8f9984ad28195fd566132a854d7573747df585d33ffe36fb3b99f129f9c36395"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The dilemma is false; the preference is defensible not as induction nor as pure logic, but as an evolutionary epistemology where 'rationality' is redefined as fitness within a knowledge-generating system. Accepting this costs the traditional notion of the rational agent as a static logician.","counterconditions":["If the evolutionary analogy fails to account for the normative 'ought' of scientific practice.","If the definition of 'fitness' in theories secretly relies on inductive probability."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Evolutionary Epistemology Transfer. Maps biological natural selection onto theory choice. 'Preference' is not a logical judgment of truth but a systemic selection mechanism. The 'cost' is abandoning the idea that science aims for certain truth, settling instead for adaptive fit.","optional_refs":["09292130faa932cf0804d2bc6f0ea0385aa2f8e73b576128b38110b22e56297b","3b6e4a5f6eb3b26ea3760f84224ff13de0d548c860da916a657705e478e673dd"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's position is internally inconsistent: he rejects induction logically but relies on it psychologically and sociologically. The preference is indefensible on his strict logical terms but unavoidable in practice, forcing a dual-system view of science that Popper refused to acknowledge.","counterconditions":["If Popper can provide a consistent logical account that accommodates the practical preference.","If the psychological reliance can be shown to be irrelevant to the logical validity of the theory."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Psychological/Sociological Gap Analysis. Identifies the rift between Popper's 'Logic of Scientific Discovery' and the actual behavior of scientists (including Popper). The mechanism posits that 'corroboration' functions as a psychological proxy for probability, despite logical prohibitions.","optional_refs":["3929e4b37e4a328b4377f432518075203e61fc9ae5cb9bb4994f2d320869639e","6940929002ab60f7d9c7dd361c7c9d91ad9a0f25db9f241c0dce29235bbb1c12"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6}],"simulation_proposals":[]}